    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Project KO11 Is Proof That Geely Wants To \"Knock Out\" Range Rover, Launches Next Year in Europe\nChevrolet's Japan-Market Corvette V8 Tourer Special Edition Is Limited to Merely 15 Units\n8 photos\nPhoto: Chevrolet\nFor an extra 1.9 million yen over the current Stingray for Japan, the V8 Tourer comes with brighter wheels, different seats, and no extra power from its already discontinued powertrain. Boasting a very coherent red, silver, and tan aesthetic, the limited-run model is coming to Japanese dealers from 21.5 million yen.\nThat's 135,750 freedom eagles at current exchange rates, and only 15 units are in the offing nationwide. Just under 10 percent more expensive than the Stingray convertible, the V8 Tourer wants you to enjoy looking at it, sitting at it, and driving it rather than explain your purchase in more rational terms.\nInstead of black, the retractable roof panel, nacelles behind the seats, A pillars, and side intake trim pieces all match the Red Mist Metallic Tincoat body panels. By default, Chevrolet Japan ships the Stingray convertible with Carbon Flash touches. Bringing those surfaces into the body color obviously changes the look of the car, especially with the roof closed, when the red no longer stops at the cabin's edge.\nSilver is there to supply the contrast, beginning with full-length Sterling Silver dual racing stripes. Chrome emblems are on the menu as well, making for a hardly anonymous combination. The lighter strips and dark-ish red work amazingly well, doing their jobs perfectly without fighting each other for the spotlight.\nThe wheels also help, for Chevrolet Japan chose Pearl Nickel forged aluminum rollers rather than Midnight Gray wheels. The design also differs, with the Pearl Nickel units rocking five split spokes instead of twenty spokes for the Grays. Black calipers are featured as well, and the visual drama continues with a familiar low-profile rear spoiler.\nPhoto: Chevrolet What Chevy refers to as Natural Dipped is the color that dominates the interior, where you will find Natural seatbelts and GT2 bucket seats. Limited-edition ornaments on the floor mats and passenger side are listed as well, yet the GT2 buckets are the real stars here, replacing the standard Competition Sport seats.\nGT2s blend comfort, a sporting appearance, carbon-fiber trim, and Nappa leather upholstery. Just like the Stingray convertible, the V8 Tourer also benefits from Magnetic Selective Ride Control dampers. The Z51 go-faster package is understandably featured as well, bringing forth an electronic limited-slip differential, better cooling, larger brakes, and so forth.\nUnder the hood, you will find the LT2 small block that General Motors recently discontinued in the US-market Corvette for the LS6. The newcomer isn't only larger, but also breathes differently because GM finally made a case for dual injection to the detriment of direct-only injection for the LT2.\nRated at 495 horsepower and 470 pound-feet (637 Newton-meters), the V8 Tourer only comes in right-hand drive. Its folding roof can be operated at speeds no higher than 30 miles per hour, which is nearly 50 clicks in metric speak. A standard front lift raises the nose roughly 1.6 inches (40 millimeters) in three seconds, and that makes a world of difference when the car meets a steep driveway.\nScheduled to arrive in customers' hands from November 2026, the V8 Tourer is backed by a three-year/60,000-kilometer warranty. Of course, Chevrolet Assist 24/7 roadside assistance is included for additional peace of mind.\nVia // Chevrolet Japan\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Corvette 2027 Chevrolet Corvette Chevrolet Japan Special Edition\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Man Buys New 2026 Chevrolet Corvette ZR1 for $221,765, Drives 698 Miles, Sells for $242,999",
